ANCESTOR: bead **etude-i19**, P1 bug, filed from the root-cause investigation of
bead etude-nad (P1). Goal: `etude init` registers
`remote.<name>.fetch = +refs/etude/*:refs/etude/*`, which makes every local run
ref a remote-tracking ref, so per `git-fetch(1)` — refs fetched due to an
explicit configured refspec are subject to pruning — **any `git fetch --prune`
deletes every run ref not yet pushed**. Measured against: a bare
`git fetch --prune` in an etude-initialised repo must not delete local
`refs/etude/*`; `etude sync` push and fetch must still work; existing repos must
have a way to drop the dangerous refspec. This destroyed three recorded gate
attempts during epic etude-9uf.

BLAST RADIUS (FIVE files): `internal/cli/init.go`, `internal/cli/init_test.go`,
`internal/cli/sync_test.go`, `docs/init.md`, `docs/sync.md`. The sync test is
the regression a gate seat asked for, pinning that `etude sync` works with no
configured etude fetch refspec — the assumption this whole bead rests on.
Nothing outside this worktree; the tests create only self-contained throwaway
repositories under `t.TempDir()`, auto-removed, and no test reads or writes the
developer's real repositories, global git config, or any shared state. It changes the
**git config `etude init` writes**, which is a behaviour change for every future
`etude init` and (by design) a one-time mutation of already-initialised repos.
It does not touch the stored manifest format, the ref layout, or any other
command. `docs/init.md` was rebased onto `origin/main` (db84538) before editing
because a sibling lane also edits it. Sibling lanes' files untouched
(`internal/cli/gate.go`, `internal/liverun/**`, `.etude/registry.yaml`,
`docs/plans/dogfood/**`, `scripts/dogfood-*.sh`, `internal/registry/**`).

TOPOLOGY: several concurrent agent lanes, each a linked git worktree, all
sharing ONE ref store in the common git dir. No daemon, no network peer. The
destructive actor is an ordinary, non-malicious git process: `git fetch --prune`,
run automatically by tooling such as `workmux remove --gone`. Because the ref
store is shared, one lane running it destroys every other lane's unpushed run
refs.

DURABILITY / DELIVERY-CLASS: the change is to **local git config**, not to any
run data. Each of the two writes is a single `git config` invocation, which git
performs as an atomic rewrite of `.git/config` under an exclusive lock, so there
is no partial state to observe within a step. Both are idempotent: a repeat run
removes nothing and converges the push entry on itself. Both go through one
helper that retries on a lock collision (git does NOT retry, and worktree lanes
share one config file), with the child under `LC_ALL=C` so the collision message
is stable regardless of locale.

AUTHORITATIVE vs PROXY identity: an entry is identified as etude-registered by
PARSING the refspec — `[+]<src>[:<dst>]` — and asking whether it has a
destination at all and whether that destination lies inside `refs/etude/`. That
is the authoritative property that makes an entry prunable. It is deliberately
not a substring test: `:refs/etude/` misses a colonless spelling, and treating a
colonless refspec as `dst = src` is WRONG for fetch, where no destination means
the ref goes to `FETCH_HEAD` and no local ref is created — so such an entry is
harmless and must not be removed. Removal then targets the exact values found,
via an anchored alternation of `regexp.QuoteMeta`-escaped literals in one
`--unset-all` call, so the set reported and the set removed cannot diverge and a
sibling refspec cannot be caught by a loose pattern.

COMPLETENESS-ENUMERATION: the guarded set is "every place a refspec into
`refs/etude/*` can be written or left behind".

| site | what it does now | covered? |
|---|---|---|
| `refspecAction` non-force path | removes hazardous fetch refspecs, then adds the push refspec | **yes** |
| `refspecAction` --force path | previously returned silently and touched nothing; now still silent on the push refspec but **still removes** a hazardous fetch refspec | **yes** — a data-loss setting is not left in place because the caller passed `--force` |
| `refspecAction` --dry-run path | previews `plan: remove <key> = <value>`, changes nothing; previewed on the force path too | **yes** |
| `--remote <other>` | init configures exactly one remote, so it cannot REPAIR another remote's config | **warned, never edited** — the safety phase applies the same exact etude-owned-refspec check to EVERY remote and reports any it finds, naming the `--remote` value needed to repair each. It does not edit a remote it was not pointed at. |
| remote missing entirely | refspec step skipped, as before | **warned** — run refs stay local-only |
| push refspec absent | nothing removes it; init adds it | **warned** if still absent |
| `etude sync` | passes `refs/etude/*:refs/etude/*` explicitly on its own fetch and push command lines (`internal/cli/sync.go:94`, `:263`) | unaffected — no config dependency |

The set is closed for this command: `refspecAction` is the only site in
`etude init` that writes remote refspec config, and the new `refspecSafetyAction`
reports every residual exposure it cannot itself repair.

IRREVERSIBLE SEQUENCES: 1

IRREVERSIBLE SEQUENCE refspec-migration: 2 steps
IRREVERSIBLE STEP refspec-migration.1: unset every value of the target remote's
fetch-refspec config key whose destination is inside the etude ref namespace,
leaving that key's other values and the remote's push-refspec key untouched.
IRREVERSIBLE STEP refspec-migration.2: converge the target remote's push-refspec
key on exactly one canonical etude push refspec, adding it when absent and
collapsing duplicates when present.

CRASH-PREFIX RESIDUALS:
CRASH-PREFIX refspec-migration.1: the target remote no longer carries an
etude-registered fetch refspec — the prune hazard is gone — and its push-refspec
key is in whatever state it was already in, which may be absent or duplicated.
The interrupted invocation prints nothing further and does NOT warn, because it
never reaches the safety phase. Run refs are safe from pruning but may not reach
the remote.

Recovery depends on HOW the command is re-run, and the two paths differ:
- A **non-force** re-run against the same remote performs step 2 and repairs the
  residual silently.
- A **`--force`** re-run does NOT. `--force` is silent on the push refspec by
  long-standing design — it regenerates scaffold files and, since this bead, also
  removes a hazardous fetch refspec, but it does not add the push refspec. An
  operator who only ever runs `--force` therefore stays in prefix 1 indefinitely.

That state is still REPORTED on every path: the safety phase runs regardless of
`--force`, so each such run prints the missing-push warning. It is repaired by
one non-force `etude init`. The residual is degraded-but-safe either way — the
prune hazard is gone; only the convenience of run refs reaching the remote is
missing — which is why removal is ordered first.
CRASH-PREFIX refspec-migration.2: the target remote carries no etude-registered
fetch refspec and exactly one canonical etude push refspec. Fully migrated.

The ordering is deliberate and is the safe one: removal first means an
interruption between the steps leaves the DANGEROUS setting gone and only a
CONVENIENCE setting unset. The reverse order would leave a window in which run
refs are still prunable. Both steps are local git-config edits this same tool
previously made, each individually atomic under git's exclusive config lock, and
each recoverable by re-running the command.

TRUST BOUNDARY: none crossed. `etude init` runs in the operator's own repository
under the operator's own account; the only inputs are the `--remote` name
(already syntactically validated upstream) and the repo's existing local git
config. No untrusted input, no network, no credential, no secret, no privilege
change. The value-pattern passed to `git config` is a compile-time constant, not
caller data.

## The three things this must do (operator-specified)

1. **Stop `init` writing the fetch refspec.**
2. **Actively remove a previously-registered one, on every init run** — every
   repo an older `etude init` has touched is exposed today and will never
   self-heal.
3. **Warn when the repo is not in the safe state**, rather than exiting quietly
   on an exposed repo.

## The one thing that must NOT happen — please check this specifically

`init.go` writes **both** `remote.<name>.fetch` and `remote.<name>.push`.

- The **fetch** refspec is the dangerous one: it makes local run refs prunable.
- The **push** refspec must **stay**: it is what makes `git push origin` carry
  run refs at all. Removing both would lose the same data by another route, and
  a change that does so is wrong.

The diff removes only from the fetch key (`fetchKey`), and
`addRefspecIfAbsent(ctx, root, pushKey, pushVal)` is unchanged. Please verify
that directly. `TestInitKeepsPushRefspecWhenRemovingFetchRefspec` pins it,
asserting the push entry survives *and* that there is exactly one of it.

## Behaviour-preserving invariants (confirm, don't reconstruct)

1. `--unset-all` is only invoked when `findEtudeFetchRefspecs` already returned a
   non-empty set, so git's exit-5 ("unset an option which does not exist") can
   never fire.
2. `findEtudeFetchRefspecs` is read-only and is the single source of the set for
   both the dry-run preview and the real removal, so the preview cannot differ
   from what happens.
3. `gitGetAll` already maps git's exit-1 (key absent) to an empty list, so a
   remote with no fetch key is not an error.
4. `refspecSafetyAction` performs no writes at all — its output is identical
   under `--dry-run` and a real run, and it runs on the force path too.
5. `statusWarn` is uncounted, like `statusNote`, so the `init:` summary counts
   are unchanged in meaning.
6. The value-pattern `:refs/etude/` contains no regex metacharacters that could
   widen the match (`/` and `:` are literal; there is no `.` or `*`).

## Verification evidence

New tests in `internal/cli/init_test.go`:

- `TestInitLeavesRunRefsSafeFromFetchPrune` — the acceptance test. Stands up a
  real bare remote, seeds the hazardous refspec, creates an **unpushed** run ref,
  and runs a **real `git fetch --prune`**. It first asserts the ref IS pruned
  (an embedded **negative control**, so the post-fix assertion cannot pass
  vacuously), then runs `init`, re-creates the ref, prunes again, and asserts it
  **survives**.
- `TestInitKeepsPushRefspecWhenRemovingFetchRefspec` — push survives, exactly one entry.
- `TestInitRemovesPreexistingEtudeFetchRefspec` — removal happens, the remote's
  ordinary `refs/heads/*` refspec is untouched, the removal is reported.
- `TestInitForceRemovesPreexistingEtudeFetchRefspec` — removal on the force path.
- `TestInitDryRunPreviewsRemovalWithoutChangingConfig` — previews and mutates nothing.
- `TestInitWarnsAboutHazardousFetchRefspecOnAnotherRemote` — warns, with the exact fix command.
- `TestInitWarnsWhenRemoteMissing` — warns, with the exact fix command.
- `TestInitEmitsNoWarningsWhenSafe` — no false warnings.

Existing tests that asserted the OLD dangerous behaviour were updated, not
deleted: they now assert the fetch key carries no etude entry, and the summary
counts moved from `2 configured` to `1 configured` (push only).

`make test`, `make lint`, `make docs-check` green repo-wide; results inlined in
the packet's verify section. Independently corroborated live on the parent repo
by the operator: unsetting only the etude fetch refspec left all 148
`refs/etude/*` refs intact through a real `git fetch --prune`, with `etude sync`
unaffected.

## Disclosed limitation — please adjudicate explicitly

A repo whose operator never re-runs `etude init` stays exposed. This bead's
answer is the documented manual one-liner in `docs/init.md` plus the warnings,
not an automatic repair from some other command. A read-only `etude doctor`
health check that reports this (and other exposures) with exact remediation
commands is tracked separately as bead **etude-ldf**. Acceptable scoping for
THIS bead, or a blocker? Justify.

